<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">travel</ansi>

Some exits between places are real journeys, not an instant step. You
set out on one the ordinary way, by going through that exit, and the
game tells you a route has begun.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Real time, not game time</ansi>

A journey runs in real time, in the background, on the server. It goes
on while you're logged out, and survives a server restart; nothing
about it fast-forwards or slows down the shared game clock. If you log
back in mid-journey, it has kept going exactly as if you'd stayed.

Along the way it spends your company's Hunger, Thirst, and Fatigue, a
little at a time rather than all at once, at a rate set by the route
and by however bad the weather is where you set out (<ansi fg="command">help weather</ansi>);
being overloaded also adds extra Fatigue cost (<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>). How
long the whole route takes is stretched by bad weather and by a heavy
load, and shortened by a mount (<ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>).
All of this is fixed the moment you set out, for that route
(<ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>). A company with a Collapsed member can't set out
(<ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Interruptions</ansi>

A route can pause you partway with something to deal with: a fallen
tree blocking the way, something that catches your eye, tracks worth
looking over, or an ambush you have to fight off (on a route that has
them: a pair of foes, unless the foe is one of the few great beasts that
hunt alone). A ranger in your company warns you of an ambush road as
you set out, and may lead you around the ambush (<ansi fg="command">help trail</ansi>).
While paused, you
choose:

  <ansi fg="command">travel resume</ansi>
  Carry on toward your destination.

  <ansi fg="command">travel return</ansi>
  Give up and head back to where you started. This ends the journey.

If the interruption is a fight, you must finish it (win, retreat, or
otherwise end it) before you can resume or return.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Arrival</ansi>

When the route's time is up, you arrive, and the companions with you
arrive beside you. One who wasn't with you when you arrived is separated
and catches up about a minute later (<ansi fg="command">help separation</ansi>). Your horses
and cargo always arrive with you. There's no way to cancel or turn back once you're moving
and nothing has interrupted you; only an interruption gives you the
chance to return early.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">travel status</ansi>
  Shows where you're headed, how far along you are and how much
  active travel time is left, and your company's current needs.

  <ansi fg="command">travel resume</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">travel return</ansi>
  Only work while a route is paused by an interruption.

Arriving can spring a random encounter, like an ordinary step into a
dangerous room (<ansi fg="command">help encounters</ansi>).

To walk step by step through rooms you have visited, see <ansi fg="command">help walkto</ansi>.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help weather</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help cargo</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help mount</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help go</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help regions</ansi> (the road east and its level bands).

An ambush you cannot evade may grant one side an opening combat round. Your best present observer, light, enemy stealth, and concealment decide it. See help ambush.
